A parabola crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-4$ and $x=2$, as shown. Find its axis of symmetry.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-7, xmax=5, ymin=-5, ymax=6, samples=100, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-4,2}, ytick={-3,3}]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, <->, domain=-5.9:3.9] {0.4*(x+4)*(x-2)};
\fill (axis cs:-4,0) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (axis cs:2,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above left] at (axis cs:-4,0) {$-4$};
\node[above right] at (axis cs:2,0) {$2$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A concave-up parabola has an $x$-intercept at $x=8$, and its axis of symmetry is the line $x=5$. Find the other $x$-intercept.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-1, xmax=10, ymin=-5, ymax=6, samples=100, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={5,8}, ytick={-3,3}]
\addplot[smooth, thick, blue, <->, domain=0.1:9.9] {0.4*(x-2)*(x-8)};
\addplot[dashed, domain=-5:6] (5,x);
\node[above right] at (axis cs:5,3) {$x=5$};
\fill (axis cs:8,0) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above right] at (axis cs:8,0) {$8$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
